00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.690 align:start position:0%
as any cat owner could probably tell you  these fuzzy fur balls spent a lot of  time grooming themselves according to  some studies up to a quarter of their  waking hours cats of sandpapery tongs  are covered in sharp little cones known  as papilla researchers wanted a closer  look at how these papillae actually help  cats groom both their outer and inner  layers of fur so that created 3d scans  of tongues from six species of cats the  domestic cat the Bobcat the Cougar Snow  Leopard tiger and lion zooming it  revealed that the papillae have a  u-shaped cavity at their tips which has  a wicking effect and helps carry  droplets of saliva through that top  layer of fur down to the cat skin the  scientists also filmed several casts  grooming when they slowed down the  footage they noticed that the cats  flared their tongues so the papillae  were perpendicular to the surface of the  tongue which maximizes how much fur they  came into contact with the researchers  also used a thermal camera and found  that depositing saliva on their skin  helps the cat's cool off if they get  overheated the researchers also designed  a hairbrush based on the shape and  configuration of the papillae on a cat's  tongue compared to a regular hair brush  the brush tug glass and was easier to  clean beyond helping humans get tangles  out of their hair or cat for another  carpets the scientists say the brush  could potentially help deliver medicine  or cleaning solution directly to cats  skin which could help improve treatments  related to cat allergies for now it's a  closer look at the surprisingly complex  everyday routine of our fuzzy feline  friends  [Music]  [Music]
 

